\n- There are $\\color{purple} 3$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/22/228c9e5eea2b5696d64d4423ecd927e12a11e6672d614cc30a947549943be0c4.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}2$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac23}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{3}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n$2$\n\nTherefore, the missing numbers are $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}2}$ and $\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{purple}3}$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142655 #edb/ref 1069 "e-15654" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142655 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7f/7fbc79b1d259f645a8296be12c2748e5abb05d02217f3628e24ef20781f06c71.png)\n\nUse the model above to determine the missing number in the following division problem.\n$\\frac{0}{0}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7f3801c4-d25f-57e0-85b3-b52df08d7072" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142655 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's start by interpreting our model:\n\n- The model shows $\\color{blue}4$ wholes. Each whole is split into *fifths*.\n\n![](images/20/2096f3451d19fc44bccca9ebe3033e9af29878aa928ad05b102e391be3f76105.png)\n\n- The fifths are grouped, and each group contains ${\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}$.\n\n![](images/96/96bc693ffe7fa65c90758e0d86a7176b19f7a279cfff12607926e0350448fe42.png)\n\n- There are $\\color{purple} 10$ groups in total.\n\n![](images/1e/1ebdc6bce6be33bbdd5fd7ff79a562c22e68103cebc8f0ded5ddac6833633989.png)\n\nSo, if we divide $\\color{blue}4$ wholes into groups of\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{\\dfrac{2}{5}}}\n$$\n\nwe get ${\\color{purple}{10}}$ groups in total.\n\nTherefore, the model represents the following division problem:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is\n\n$$\n\\bbox[3pt, Gainsboro]{\\color{red}\\dfrac{2}{5}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142845 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142845 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050665 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186142